{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "לשמוע אל בלעם", :translit "lishmo'a el bil'am", :gloss "to listen to Balaam"} {:he "לא תדרש שלומם", :translit "lo tidrosh shlomam", :gloss "you shall not seek their peace"} {:he "לא תתעב אדומי", :translit "lo tit'ev adomi", :gloss "you shall not abominate the Edomite"} {:he "וישטום עשו את יעקב", :translit "vayishtom esav et ya'akov", :gloss "and Esau hated Jacob"} {:he "כי עמונים לא קדמו אותך בלחם ובמים", :translit "ki amonim lo kidmu otcha balechem uvmayim", :gloss "for the Ammonites did not greet you with bread and water"} {:he "כאשר עשו לי בני עשו", :translit "ka'asher asu li bnei esav", :gloss "as the children of Esau did to me"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "semikhut parshiyot: the adjacency of 'you shall not abominate the Edomite' to the Ammonite/Moabite prohibition yields the contrast that Edom gets better treatment because Esau greeted Israel with bread and water."}], :frame {:text "The principle of semikhut parshiyot (adjacency of verses or passages) draws legal or narrative inference from the placement of one section next to another. The prohibition against seeking the peace of Ammon and Moab is linked to the story of Balaam, whom they hired, as discussed at Sanhedrin 40b (= 'HaNezikin 40b' in the printing the Ba'al HaTurim cites). The phrase 'Esau hated Jacob' appears at Genesis 27:41, and Esau's greeting of Israel 'as the children of Esau did to me' is recalled at Deuteronomy 2:29.", :sources ["Sanhedrin 40b" "Genesis 27:41" "Deuteronomy 2:29" "Deuteronomy 23:5-6" "Deuteronomy 23:8"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "semikhut parshiyot: the adjacency of 'you shall not abominate the Edomite' to the Ammonite/Moabite prohibition yields the contrast that Edom gets better treatment because Esau greeted Israel with bread and water.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:38:33.801095132Z", :model "glm-5.2"}